   <xsd:complexType name="ExpertModeType">
      <xsd:annotation>
         <xsd:documentation>Expert/developer settings for advanced calculations and file output control.</xsd:documentation>
      </xsd:annotation>
      <xsd:attribute name="gw" type="xsd:integer" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>GW mode flag for many-body perturbation theory calculations.</xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
      <xsd:attribute name="spex" type="xsd:integer" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>SPEX interface flag for GW calculations with SPEX code.</xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
      <xsd:attribute default="NONE" name="vdW" type="xsd:string" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>
               Van der Waals (dispersion) correction method.
               
               Standard DFT functionals (LDA, GGA) fail to describe long-range
               correlation effects responsible for van der Waals interactions.
               Several correction schemes available:
               
               Available Methods:
               
               "NONE" (default):
               No vdW correction. Standard DFT only.
               - Appropriate for covalent/ionic bonding
               - May underestimate binding in layered materials, molecular crystals
               
               "D3" or "DFT-D3" (Grimme D3):
               Semi-empirical pairwise dispersion correction by Grimme (2010).
               - E_disp = -½ Σ_ij f_damp(R_ij) · C6_ij/R_ij^6
               - C6 coefficients: Element and environment-dependent
               - Damping function f_damp: Avoids double-counting at short range
               - Most popular choice: Robust, well-tested for diverse systems
               - Compatible with most GGA functionals (PBE, RPBE, revPBE)
               
               Implementation: Add pairwise -C6/R^6 correction to total energy.
               Analytic forces and stress available.
               
               "Dion":
               Non-local van der Waals density functional (vdW-DF).
               - E_c^nl[n] = ∫∫ n(r)·Φ(r,r')·n(r') dr dr'
               - Φ: Non-local correlation kernel
               - Energy only: No contribution to KS potential
               - Use for: Energy comparisons, structural optimization
               
               "Dion,pot":
               vdW-DF with non-local potential.
               - Includes vdW contribution in Kohn-Sham potential: δE_c^nl/δn(r)
               - Self-consistent vdW effects on charge density
               - More accurate but significantly more expensive
               - Recommended for: Electronic structure, charge transfer, polarizability
               
               "Dion,core":
               vdW-DF with core charge correction.
               - Subtracts frozen core contribution from vdW kernel
               - Avoids unphysical core-core vdW interactions
               - Use when: Core-valence partitioning important (heavy elements)
               
               Method Selection Guide:
               
               1. Molecular crystals, layered materials (graphite, MoS2):
                  → Use D3 for balance of accuracy and efficiency
               
               2. Weakly bound complexes, physisorption:
                  → D3 for quick estimates
                  → Dion,pot for accurate electronic structure
               
               3. Liquid water, biological systems:
                  → D3 widely validated
               
               4. Metal surfaces with adsorption:
                  → Dion methods (account for non-local screening)
               
               5. Heavy-element systems (Au, Pt):
                  → Consider Dion,core to avoid core artifacts
               
               vdW_tol Parameter:
               Controls convergence tolerance for vdW energy summation.
               - Default: 0.0005 Hartree
               - Decrease for high-precision calculations
               - Sum includes many distant pairs; check convergence with system size
               
               Important Considerations:
               - D3: Requires parameterization for each functional (PBE-D3, RPBE-D3, etc.)
               - Dion methods: More expensive, may affect SCF convergence
               - Always report which vdW method used when publishing
               - Some functionals include built-in vdW (vdW-DF-cx not via this keyword)
               - For layered materials: Compare binding curves with and without vdW
               
               Output:
               vdW energy contribution reported separately in total energy.
               Check OUT file for: E_vdW, contributions by atom pairs.
            </xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
      <xsd:attribute default="0.0005" name="vdW_tol" type="FleurDouble" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>Tolerance for vdW sum convergence.</xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
      <xsd:attribute default="F" name="pot8" type="FleurBool" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>Write potential in new pot8 format.</xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
      <xsd:attribute default="F" name="eig66" type="FleurBool" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>Write eigenvalues in old eig66 format.</xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
      <xsd:attribute default="0" name="lpr" type="xsd:integer" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>Print level for debugging output.</xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
      <xsd:attribute default="99" name="isec1" type="xsd:integer" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>Secondary variational step control.</xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
      <xsd:attribute default="F" name="secvar" type="FleurBool" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>Enable secondary variational step for spin-orbit coupling.</xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
      <xsd:attribute default="1.0" name="warp_factor" type="FleurDouble" use="optional">
         <xsd:annotation>
            <xsd:documentation>Warp factor for potential/density mixing in warped cell calculations.</xsd:documentation>
         </xsd:annotation>
      </xsd:attribute>
   </xsd:complexType>

